A||||5||Master_Sushi||2.5 knocking on startup -||||5||
Z||000000||Master_Sushi||12-05-2001||12:37 AM||||Recently when I start my 84se in the morning when its cold, I get a loud knocking or pinging sound for about the first 5-15 secs its ran. Its only when its cold and dosen't happen all the time. Its really not bothering me much, I just want to know what it is.<P>Only thing I can think of is maybe the timing is off? Oil pump is dieing? Or is the engine on its way out?? The car seems to be runing well otherwise. I also run Mobil 1 15w-50, it seems to like this oil best. Car is just about to hit 100,000 miles.<P><BR>I did to a search and didn't find a whole lot that helps me either.<P>Thanks<P><BR>EDIT... I forgot to add it did this before I changed to mobil1 15w-50. So I dont think thats causing it, if anything it helped some. Engine sounds great once it warms up. It pulls nicely all the way up to 5000rpm.<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||SKIDMARK||12-05-2001||12:46 AM||||15w-50? The reason they reccomend the 5w-30 is that they had a problem burning oil with the higher viscosity. The thicker oil sticks to the cylinder walls and the rings don't wipe it off as well. As for the knocking, I had a 3.8 in a wagon that did that for 3 years before I sold it. I thought the oil pump was getting tired but replacing it made no difference. I think maybe the crank bearings had a little too much clearance.||||reg||1
Z||000002||California Kid||12-05-2001||12:49 AM||||Your hydralic lifters are what's causing the noise and 15w50 is to heavy for cold temperatures. You should be using 5w30 for fall, spring, and winter.<P>If going to a lighter wt oil doesn't solve it the lifter could be vanished up or just not working that great anymore.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/californiakidsig_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||12-05-2001||12:56 AM||||That was quick!<P>It was doing this before I switched to mobil one. I use to run *really* cheap 5w-30(superteck from walleyworld  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ). But I changed to the 15w-50 thinking maybe thicker higher quality synthetic oil would fix it. I guess not though   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. I did notice the engine is much more quiet and smoother with the mobil 1  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon14.gif">  .<P>Next oil change maybe I'll try mobil 1 5w-30.<P>Would bad lifters or to much clearance in crank bearings cause any big engine probs?<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||12-05-2001||09:11 AM||||If this were a normal winter, I'd recommend trying the Mobil 1 0W-30. I've run this stuff year round in several cars and it's great.<P>But, then again, now that Buffalo is in the tropics again (so is Sioux City, for that matter), 5W-30 is fine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||California Kid||12-05-2001||10:56 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Master_Sushi:<BR>[B]Would bad lifters or to much clearance in crank bearings cause any big engine probs?<BR>*******************************************<BR>I don't think you have a serious problem considering the mileage on the engine. Hydralic lifters "pump up" with oil pressure at start up, yours are just a little bit slow at doing it. The lighter weight oil/syntheic just may cure this condition, but there are other factors that can contribute to this condition. Most important is that your oil pressure is not too low, if you're getting Ok oil pressure, I wouldn't worry about it. Still use the lighter oil for winter, as most engine wear occurs at engine start up, you want the oil to get everywhere as soon as possible.<P>||||reg||1
